# Existential Stack Configuration
# Run existential.sh script, or copy this file to global.env and fill in your values

#==================================================#
#               Global Configuration               #
#==================================================#

# Service Enablement Configuration
# Set to 'true' to enable a service, 'false' to disable
# Each service corresponds to a subdirectory with docker-compose.yml

# AI Services
EXIST_IS_AI_CHATTERBOX=false
EXIST_IS_AI_COMFYUI=false
EXIST_IS_AI_FIRECRAWL=false
EXIST_IS_AI_HERMES=false
EXIST_IS_AI_HONCHO=false
EXIST_IS_AI_MCP=false
EXIST_IS_AI_OLLAMA=false
EXIST_IS_AI_OPEN_WEBUI=false
EXIST_IS_AI_OPENVIKING=false
EXIST_IS_AI_WHISPERX=false
EXIST_IS_AI_WYOMING_PIPER=false
EXIST_IS_AI_WYOMING_WHISPER=false

# Hosting Services
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_CADDY=true
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_GRAFANA=false
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_LOKI=false
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_PIHOLE=false
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_PORTAINER=false
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_PROMETHEUS=false
EXIST_IS_HOSTING_UPTIME_KUMA=false

# NAS Services
EXIST_IS_NAS_COLLABORA=false
EXIST_IS_NAS_MINIO=false
EXIST_IS_NAS_NEXTCLOUD=false
EXIST_IS_NAS_REDIS=false

# Application Services
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_ACTUAL_BUDGET=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_APPSMITH=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_CODE_SERVER=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_DASHY=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_DECREE=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_IMMICH=false # Recommended to run this directly from the immich directory (not here)
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_IT_TOOLS=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_LOWCODER=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_MEALIE=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_NOCODB=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_NTFY=false
EXIST_IS_SERVICES_HOMEASSISTANT=false

#==================================================#
#                  Model Selection                 #
#==================================================#

# One place to name the models the whole stack uses. Everything that talks to
# ollama reads these rather than hardcoding a tag: the ollama-decree migrations
# that pull them, honcho's deriver/dialectic/embedder (ai/honcho/config.toml),
# openviking's vector store (ai/openviking/.env), and hermes' own config.
#
# You do not normally edit this block by hand. `./existential.sh` asks how much
# VRAM the machine has on first run and fills it in; change your mind later with
#
#     ./existential.sh run models
#
# The tier table behind that lives in src/utils/model-tiers.sh, with the
# reasoning for every size. The values below are the 8 GB tier — the default,
# and the baseline this stack now targets.
#
# After changing anything here:
#     ./existential.sh                        # re-renders honcho's config
#     ./existential.sh run ollama pull-models
#
# ── The budget ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On one card, at all times:
#
#     chat model  +  bge-m3 (1.2 GB)  +  KV cache  <  your VRAM
#
# Measured downloads, so you can sanity-check a swap:
#
#   qwen3-vl:2b              1.9 GB    tools, vision    CPU-only tier
#   qwen3-vl:4b              3.3 GB    tools, vision      6 GB tier
#   gemma4:e2b-it-qat        4.3 GB    tools, vision      8 GB tier
#   gemma4:e4b-it-qat        6.1 GB    tools, vision     12 GB tier
#   gemma4:12b-it-qat        7.2 GB    tools, vision     16 GB tier
#   gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat     16 GB    tools, vision     24 GB tier
#   gemma4:31b-it-bf16        63 GB    tools, vision     96 GB tier (bf16)
#   bge-m3                   1.2 GB    embeddings        every tier
#
# Everything up to 24 GB is quantised; the 96 GB tier is the only one that runs
# the weights at full precision, which is what that much memory actually buys.
#
# Two traps worth knowing:
#
#   gemma4's "E" tags. The E in e2b/e4b means *effective* parameters — a claim
#   about compute, not file size. gemma4:e4b is an 8B model that downloads at
#   9.6 GB, and gemma4:e2b at 7.2 GB. The -qat (quantization-aware trained)
#   variants above are the ones that actually fit; the plain q4 tags do not.
#
#   Mixture-of-experts sizes. nemotron-3.5-lightning is 30B with only 3B active
#   per token, which sounds small and is not: every expert stays resident, so it
#   is a 25 GB model that merely runs at 3B speed.
#
# Whatever you pick, the chat model MUST have ollama's "tools" capability or
# hermes can talk but not act. Every tier tag is also multimodal, so images go
# to the model already loaded instead of evicting it for a separate llava.
#
# Speech-to-text and text-to-speech deliberately run on CPU (wyoming-whisper,
# wyoming-piper) so they never compete with the LLM for VRAM.

# Which GPU this machine has: nvidia | amd | none.
#
# This is the FIRST question quest asks, and its presence is the record of
# having asked — quest never re-asks once it is set, and `./existential.sh run
# models` is the way back. It SHIPS BLANK for the same reason EXIST_VRAM_GB
# does: a value here means the first run never gets to ask.
#
# What it changes, all in src/generate-compose.ts:
#
#   nvidia  nothing — the templates already declare the nvidia device
#           reservation, which is correct for the majority case.
#   amd     the nvidia reservation is stripped and each service's
#           `x-exist-gpu.amd` block is merged in instead (Vulkan/ROCm).
#   none    the nvidia reservation is stripped and `x-exist-gpu.none` merged.
#
# The strip is not cosmetic: docker refuses to create a container whose device
# driver it cannot satisfy, so one nvidia-reserving service takes down
# `docker compose up` for the whole stack on a host without that runtime.
#
# Blank is treated as nvidia, which is what every install that predates this
# setting already assumed.
EXIST_GPU_VENDOR=

# The tier that was picked. Its presence is also the record of having asked —
# quest never re-asks once this is set. Blank re-asks on the next quest run,
# which is why it SHIPS BLANK: a value here (it used to ship 8) means the first
# run never gets to ask, and every machine silently inherits an 8 GB GPU.
#
# The model values below still ship at the default tier, so a non-interactive
# install is complete without answering anything. Only this line is the question.
#
# 0 is the CPU-only tier. You normally reach it by answering "No GPU" to the
# vendor question above, which sets this to 0 for you and skips the VRAM
# question — a VRAM number means nothing without a card. Expect seconds per
# token, not tokens per second; the wyoming voice services are unaffected,
# they are CPU already.
EXIST_VRAM_GB=

# Primary chat / reasoning model. Used by hermes and open-webui.
EXIST_MODEL_CHAT=gemma4:e2b-it-qat

# Context window for the chat model. Hermes sends a large system prompt
# (skills + memory); ollama's stock 4096–8192 silently truncates it, which
# reads as the model ignoring its instructions rather than as an error. The
# ollama-decree migration rebuilds EXIST_MODEL_CHAT with this num_ctx baked in,
# keeping the same tag. Bigger context costs VRAM — the KV cache grows with it.
EXIST_MODEL_CHAT_NUM_CTX=32768

# Background extraction model — honcho's deriver, dialectic reasoning and
# session summaries. The same tag as chat, so only ONE model stays resident.
# Split them only if you have VRAM for two models at once.
EXIST_MODEL_EXTRACT=gemma4:e2b-it-qat

# Vision model — OCR (the ollama-ocr file processor) and image chat in Open
# WebUI. Also the same tag: every tier model is multimodal, so an image reuses
# what is already loaded. Set it blank to disable image handling entirely.
EXIST_MODEL_VISION=gemma4:e2b-it-qat

# Embedding model for openviking's vector store.
#
# WARNING: changing this after first ingestion corrupts the vector index — the
# dimensions no longer match what is already stored. Wipe
# volumes_local/openviking_data if you do. This is why it is the one model that
# does NOT vary by tier.
EXIST_MODEL_EMBED=bge-m3

# Must match the embedding model above: bge-m3 → 1024, nomic-embed-text → 768,
# mxbai-embed-large → 1024.
EXIST_MODEL_EMBED_DIM=1024

# Speech-to-text model for Home Assistant voice (wyoming-whisper, CPU).
# tiny | base | small | medium | large-v3 — see ai/wyoming-whisper/.env for the
# latency/accuracy trade-off. This is NOT whisperx: that one runs on the GPU and
# transcribes long recordings with speaker labels (the auto-transcription quest).
EXIST_MODEL_STT=base

# Language hint for the STT model. Pinning it beats auto-detect on short
# commands, which is all HA voice sends.
EXIST_MODEL_STT_LANGUAGE=en

# Text-to-speech voice for Home Assistant (wyoming-piper, CPU).
# <lang>_<REGION>-<name>-<quality>; browse and listen at
# https://rhasspy.github.io/piper-samples/
EXIST_MODEL_TTS_VOICE=en_US-lessac-medium

# Set to true if the 'exist' Docker network is created externally (e.g. via docker network create)
# Set to false to have it created and managed by Docker Compose
EXIST_NETWORK_EXTERNAL=false

# Default email to use in services
EXIST_EMAIL=EXIST_CLI

# Default username to use in services
EXIST_USERNAME=EXIST_CLI
# Default password to use in services
EXIST_PASSWORD=EXIST_24_CHAR_PASSWORD

# Host user/group IDs. Every container runs as this uid:gid (referenced in service
# compose files as ${EXIST_PUID:-1000}:${EXIST_PGID:-1000}), so files written into
# bind-mounted volumes stay owned by — and deletable by — the user running the stack.
# existential.sh auto-detects and writes these (from `id -u`/`id -g`) on first run if
# absent, defaulting to 1000. Override here only if your containers must run as a
# different user than the one invoking docker compose.
# EXIST_PUID=1000
# EXIST_PGID=1000

# IP of the machine running caddy — the TAILNET address when tailscale is up,
# otherwise the LAN address. Must stay ABOVE EXIST_DOMAIN: the EXIST_NIP_DOMAIN
# placeholder below is derived from this value, and placeholders resolve top-down.
#
# EXIST_HOST_IP is filled in automatically — existential.sh runs `tailscale ip -4`
# and falls back to the source address the kernel would use to reach the internet,
# then passes the result to the renderer. It was a prompt once; a blank answer sent
# EXIST_DOMAIN to x.internal, which nothing resolves, so the first run ended on a
# DNS warning. Set a literal IP here instead if you want a different interface.
#
# Tailscale is preferred because its address reaches every device you enrolled
# rather than only this LAN, and it does not change when the machine moves
# networks. Nothing binds to this value; it only feeds EXIST_NIP_DOMAIN below,
# pihole's wildcard record, and the EXIST_PEER_HOST_IP default.
EXIST_LOCAL_HOST_IP=EXIST_HOST_IP

# Base domain for every `<slug>.<domain>` hostname. Caddy fronts each one.
#
# The default resolves to EXIST_NIP_DOMAIN — `<your-ip-with-dashes>.nip.io`, e.g.
# 100-101-102-103.nip.io. nip.io is public wildcard DNS that maps that name straight
# back to the IP, so `https://dashy.<domain>` works from any phone or laptop with
# NO pihole and NO /etc/hosts. Spin up and it just works.
#
# With tailscale up that IP is your tailnet address, so the name resolves publicly
# but only ROUTES from inside your tailnet — private by construction, reachable
# from anywhere you are logged in, and no tailnet DNS configuration at all.
#
# NEVER set this to a MagicDNS name like <node>.ts.net. MagicDNS resolves exact
# node names only — there is no wildcard beneath one — so every <slug>. under it
# is NXDOMAIN. The tailnet IP is what you want; nip.io supplies the wildcard.
#
# Trade-off: name resolution depends on an internet DNS lookup. Enabling pihole
# later removes that (its single wildcard record answers locally) — pihole is an
# upgrade rather than a requirement. Nothing about container-to-container traffic
# is affected either way; that always uses Docker DNS.
#
# DNS and TLS are independent choices — changing one does not require the other:
#   x.internal              pihole (or /etc/hosts) required; fully offline, and a
#                           second stack on the LAN can take y.internal.
#   a domain you own        e.g. local.example.com. NO public A record and NO
#                           inbound connectivity needed: let pihole resolve it on
#                           the LAN, and issue a wildcard cert over the DNS-01
#                           challenge (Let's Encrypt reads a TXT record from public
#                           DNS; it never connects to you). Drop the cert into
#                           hosting/caddy/certs/ as internal.pem/internal-key.pem —
#                           caddy's exist.initial.sh skips minting when one exists.
#                           This is the only setup with no cert-trust step, and it
#                           still works with the WAN unplugged between renewals.
EXIST_DOMAIN=EXIST_NIP_DOMAIN

# IP of the machine running the services, when caddy lives on a separate
# front-door host (peer mode). The front-door Caddy forwards every
# `<slug>.<domain>` to this IP, where a second Caddy routes to the right container.
# Optional — blank defaults to LOCAL_HOST_IP (single-host / co-located).
# DEFAULT_FROM: EXIST_LOCAL_HOST_IP
EXIST_PEER_HOST_IP=

# Public domain for legit (Let's Encrypt) certs alongside the .internal
# hostnames. Blank = local-only, no public-domain blocks generated. When set
# (e.g. homelab.example.com), Caddy will additionally serve every service at
# <slug>.<EXIST_PUBLIC_DOMAIN> with real ACME certs. Requires:
#   - You own the domain
#   - <domain> and *.<domain> resolve to your public IP
#   - Ports 80 + 443 forwarded from your router to this machine
#     (80 is required for the ACME HTTP-01 challenge)
# Run ./existential.sh setup caddy to (re)generate Caddyfile.public after
# changing this value.
EXIST_PUBLIC_DOMAIN=

# Hermes gateway API key — shared by hermes-agent, and open-webui
EXIST_HERMES_API_KEY=EXIST_32_CHAR_HEX_KEY


# NTFY (shared across services)
# Container-to-container URL (Docker DNS). Token is generated by
# ./existential.sh run ntfy setup after first boot.
EXIST_NTFY_URL=http://ntfy:80
EXIST_NTFY_TOKEN=

# Telegram fallback — used by notify.sh when ntfy is unreachable
# Create a bot: https://t.me/BotFather → /newbot
# Get chat ID: message the bot, then https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
EXIST_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
EXIST_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# Docker network subnet (must match your docker network create command)
EXIST_DOCKER_NETWORK_SUBNET=10.0.0.0/24

#==================================================#
#                 NAS Configuration                #
#==================================================#

# Persistent data is stored as host bind mounts — never Docker-managed volumes.
# Without an NFS host mount below, every volume lives under <repo>/volumes/<name>.
#
# All three are blank by default and none of them is a first-run question: a NAS
# is a thing you either have or you don't, and being asked to press Enter three
# times to say "no" is not setup. The NAS Storage quest
# (./existential.sh quest) walks through mounting the export and filling these
# in, and you can run it at any time — nothing here is one-way.
#
# To do it by hand: mount the NFS export on the HOST (fstab/autofs), then set
# EXIST_NFS_HOST_MOUNT to that mountpoint. SERVER_ADDRESS/BASE_PATH below only
# document where the export lives (used by the quest); Docker no longer mounts
# NFS itself.

# Host mountpoint of the NFS export (where the NAS is mounted on THIS machine).
# EG /mnt/nas — blank keeps all data under <repo>/volumes/.
EXIST_NFS_HOST_MOUNT=
# NFS Server IP Address (documentation / quest only)
# EG 192.168.1.8
EXIST_NFS_SERVER_ADDRESS=
# NFS Server Share Base Path (documentation / quest only)
# EG /mnt/container_data
EXIST_NFS_BASE_PATH=

# Backup destination — any rclone remote ("<remote>:<path>"). Set via
# ./existential.sh setup backup after configuring rclone. Leave blank to
# disable scheduled DB backups; volume backups are invoked manually or via
# host cron (./existential.sh backup volumes [tier]).
EXIST_BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE=

# EG: minio-api.x.internal
EXIST_MINIO_DOMAIN=
# EG: https://minio-api.x.internal
EXIST_MINIO_SERVER_URL=

# Bearer token minIO uses when posting to the decree /minio webhook endpoint
EXIST_DECREE_MINIO_WEBHOOK_AUTH_TOKEN=EXIST_32_CHAR_HEX_KEY

# EG: https://nextcloud.x.internal
EXIST_NEXTCLOUD_DOMAIN=

EXIST_REDIS_PASSWORD=EXIST_24_CHAR_PASSWORD
